From 8a27a4fc538ca2b5c1c2001b0b28c826dfc1092c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: anonymous <anonymous@invalid>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Keep music at normal speed while fast-forwarding / in
 slow motion

When fast-forwarding, melonDS runs the whole console faster, so music
and sound effects play faster and higher. This adds an option to keep
them at their normal speed and pitch while the game itself runs fast
(or slow).

How it works: DS games play music on the ARM7. A periodic OS alarm,
driven by the ARM7 hardware timers, steps the sound sequencer about
every 5.2 ms, and the SPU plays the notes. A new "sound clock" runs the
ARM7 timers and the SPU (channels, capture units and the output
timeline) at a fraction of emulated time. While fast-forward or slow
motion is active, the frontend sets that fraction to 1/ratio every
frame, where ratio is how much faster than real time the emulation is
actually running (capped by the fast-forward target and nudged by the
output buffer fill). The sound hardware then runs on real time while
the ARM9 and everything else run at the chosen speed.

Option: Audio settings > "Keep music at normal speed when
fast-forwarding / in slow motion" (Audio.KeepMusicSpeed, default on).
With the option off, or at normal speed, the code paths are unchanged.

Notes:
- Waits on sound (e.g. a game waiting for a cry to finish) take real
  time, so those moments are not sped up.
- DSi-mode DSP audio is not covered.
